('node-3-1', 'phase-03-lesson-01-algorithmic-complexity-big-o-intuition', 'module-5', 1, 'Lesson 5.1: Algorithmic Complexity & Big-O Intuition', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Phase 2', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0, Phase 2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 810.0, 50.0, '# Lesson 3.1: Algorithmic Complexity & Big-O Intuition

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0, Phase 2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.1.1` Why algorithmic efficiency matters: code that works on 10 items can completely freeze on 1,000,000 items.
  - `3.1.2` Measuring scale, not clock time: why benchmarking milliseconds is misleading across different hardware.
  - `3.1.3` The Big-O notation mental model: describing how runtime grows as input size N increases.
  - `3.1.4` The Big-O hierarchy: O(1) constant, O(log N) logarithmic, O(N) linear, O(N log N), O(N^2) quadratic.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Confusing the best-case runtime with worst-case or average-case Big-O guarantees.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Analyze three code snippets and write down their exact Big-O time and space complexity with justification.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Complexity auditing.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.1: Algorithmic Complexity & Big-O Intuition\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Analyze three code snippets and write down their exact Big-O time and space complexity with justific\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.1", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Analyze three code snippets and write down their exact Big-O time and space complexity with justification.", "subtopics": ["3.1.1 Why algorithmic efficiency matters: code that works on 10 items can completely freeze on 1,000,000 items.", "3.1.2 Measuring scale, not clock time: why benchmarking milliseconds is misleading across different hardware.", "3.1.3 The Big-O notation mental model: describing how runtime grows as input size N increases.", "3.1.4 The Big-O hierarchy: O(1) constant, O(log N) logarithmic, O(N) linear, O(N log N), O(N^2) quadratic."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Confusing the best-case runtime with worst-case or average-case Big-O guarantees.", "How does Algorithmic Complexity & Big-O Intuition scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-2', 'phase-03-lesson-02-constant-o-1-vs-linear-o-n-complexity', 'module-5', 2, 'Lesson 5.2: Constant O(1) vs Linear O(N) Complexity', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.1', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.1 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 870.0, 100.0, '# Lesson 3.2: Constant O(1) vs Linear O(N) Complexity

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.1
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.2.1` Constant time O(1): operations that take the exact same amount of time regardless of dataset size.
  - `3.2.2` Examples of O(1): looking up an array index, appending to a list, looking up a key in a dictionary.
  - `3.2.3` Linear time O(N): operations whose execution time doubles whenever the input dataset doubles.
  - `3.2.4` Examples of O(N): scanning an unsorted list with for, calculating sum(list), counting character matches.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Accidentally placing an O(N) operation inside a loop, unintentionally creating a quadratic O(N^2) disaster.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Benchmark array index lookup vs linear scan across 10, 1,000, and 1,000,000 elements to prove O(1) vs O(N).
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Constant-time buffer indexing.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.2: Constant O(1) vs Linear O(N) Complexity\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Benchmark array index lookup vs linear scan across 10, 1,000, and 1,000,000 elements to prove O(1) v\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.2", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Benchmark array index lookup vs linear scan across 10, 1,000, and 1,000,000 elements to prove O(1) vs O(N).", "subtopics": ["3.2.1 Constant time O(1): operations that take the exact same amount of time regardless of dataset size.", "3.2.2 Examples of O(1): looking up an array index, appending to a list, looking up a key in a dictionary.", "3.2.3 Linear time O(N): operations whose execution time doubles whenever the input dataset doubles.", "3.2.4 Examples of O(N): scanning an unsorted list with for, calculating sum(list), counting character matches."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Accidentally placing an O(N) operation inside a loop, unintentionally creating a quadratic O(N^2) disaster.", "How does Constant O(1) vs Linear O(N) Complexity scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-3', 'phase-03-lesson-03-quadratic-o-n-2-the-nested-loop-trap', 'module-5', 3, 'Lesson 5.3: Quadratic O(N^2) & The Nested Loop Trap', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 750.0, 150.0, '# Lesson 3.3: Quadratic O(N^2) & The Nested Loop Trap

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.3.1` The nested loop trap: running a loop of size N inside another loop of size N.
  - `3.3.2` Why O(N^2) breaks at scale: processing 1,000 items takes 1,000,000 steps; 100,000 items takes 10 billion steps!
  - `3.3.3` Common hidden nested loops: calling item in list or list.count() inside an outer for loop.
  - `3.3.4` How to identify quadratic bottlenecks in code reviews before they reach production.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Writing nested loops to check for duplicates across two lists instead of using a hash set.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Take an O(N^2) duplicate finder and measure its runtime on a 50,000-item list.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Identifying algorithmic bottlenecks.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.3: Quadratic O(N^2) & The Nested Loop Trap\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Take an O(N^2) duplicate finder and measure its runtime on a 50,000-item list.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.3", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Take an O(N^2) duplicate finder and measure its runtime on a 50,000-item list.", "subtopics": ["3.3.1 The nested loop trap: running a loop of size N inside another loop of size N.", "3.3.2 Why O(N^2) breaks at scale: processing 1,000 items takes 1,000,000 steps; 100,000 items takes 10 billion steps!", "3.3.3 Common hidden nested loops: calling item in list or list.count() inside an outer for loop.", "3.3.4 How to identify quadratic bottlenecks in code reviews before they reach production."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Writing nested loops to check for duplicates across two lists instead of using a hash set.", "How does Quadratic O(N^2) & The Nested Loop Trap scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-4', 'phase-03-lesson-04-logarithmic-o-log-n-divide-and-conquer', 'module-5', 4, 'Lesson 5.4: Logarithmic O(log N) & Divide and Conquer', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.1', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.1 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 810.0, 200.0, '# Lesson 3.4: Logarithmic O(log N) & Divide and Conquer

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.1
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.4.1` The power of cutting problems in half: why dividing search spaces is so blindingly fast.
  - `3.4.2` The phone book analogy: finding a name by opening to the middle and discarding half the book.
  - `3.4.3` Logarithmic scaling: searching 1,000 items takes 10 steps; searching 1,000,000,000 items takes only 30 steps!
  - `3.4.4` Identifying divide-and-conquer algorithms in real-world systems (binary search, balanced trees).
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Applying binary search to an unsorted list without sorting it first, producing completely wrong results.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Calculate how many comparison steps binary search needs to locate an item in a dataset of 4 billion records.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Scalable search mechanics.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.4: Logarithmic O(log N) & Divide and Conquer\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Calculate how many comparison steps binary search needs to locate an item in a dataset of 4 billion \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.4", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Calculate how many comparison steps binary search needs to locate an item in a dataset of 4 billion records.", "subtopics": ["3.4.1 The power of cutting problems in half: why dividing search spaces is so blindingly fast.", "3.4.2 The phone book analogy: finding a name by opening to the middle and discarding half the book.", "3.4.3 Logarithmic scaling: searching 1,000 items takes 10 steps; searching 1,000,000,000 items takes only 30 steps!", "3.4.4 Identifying divide-and-conquer algorithms in real-world systems (binary search, balanced trees)."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Applying binary search to an unsorted list without sorting it first, producing completely wrong results.", "How does Logarithmic O(log N) & Divide and Conquer scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-5', 'phase-03-lesson-05-two-pointers-opposing-direction-convergin', 'module-5', 5, 'Lesson 5.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 870.0, 250.0, '# Lesson 3.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.5.1` The two-pointer technique: using two coordinate indices simultaneously to inspect an array.
  - `3.5.2` Opposing pointers: starting Left at index 0 and Right at index N-1, stepping inward toward each other.
  - `3.5.3` Solving Two-Sum on sorted arrays: moving Left when sum is too small, moving Right when sum is too large.
  - `3.5.4` Why two pointers turns an O(N^2) brute-force nested search into an optimal single-pass O(N) solution.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Pointer crossing bugs: forgetting the while left < right boundary condition, causing pointers to cross.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) time.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Paired token matching.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) t\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.5", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) time.", "subtopics": ["3.5.1 The two-pointer technique: using two coordinate indices simultaneously to inspect an array.", "3.5.2 Opposing pointers: starting Left at index 0 and Right at index N-1, stepping inward toward each other.", "3.5.3 Solving Two-Sum on sorted arrays: moving Left when sum is too small, moving Right when sum is too large.", "3.5.4 Why two pointers turns an O(N^2) brute-force nested search into an optimal single-pass O(N) solution."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Pointer crossing bugs: forgetting the while left < right boundary condition, causing pointers to cross.", "How does Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-6', 'phase-03-lesson-06-two-pointers-fast-slow-pointer-cycle-dete', 'module-5', 6, 'Lesson 5.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.5', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.5 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 750.0, 300.0, '# Lesson 3.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.5
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.6.1` Same-direction pointers: two pointers starting at the beginning and moving forward at different speeds.
  - `3.6.2` The Tortoise and Hare algorithm: slow pointer moves 1 step while fast pointer moves 2 steps.
  - `3.6.3` Cycle detection intuition: if a runner and a walker circle a closed track, the runner will eventually lap the walker.
  - `3.6.4` Applications: detecting infinite loops, finding list midpoints, and cycle detection in state graphs.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Dereferencing null pointer in fast.next.next without checking if fast or fast.next is None.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite loop.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Agent loop cycle detection.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite l\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.6", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite loop.", "subtopics": ["3.6.1 Same-direction pointers: two pointers starting at the beginning and moving forward at different speeds.", "3.6.2 The Tortoise and Hare algorithm: slow pointer moves 1 step while fast pointer moves 2 steps.", "3.6.3 Cycle detection intuition: if a runner and a walker circle a closed track, the runner will eventually lap the walker.", "3.6.4 Applications: detecting infinite loops, finding list midpoints, and cycle detection in state graphs."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Dereferencing null pointer in fast.next.next without checking if fast or fast.next is None.", "How does Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection) scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-7', 'phase-03-lesson-07-sliding-window-fixed-size-subarrays', 'module-5', 7, 'Lesson 5.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 810.0, 350.0, '# Lesson 3.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.7.1` The sliding window paradigm: maintaining a visible slice of size K across a continuous stream of data.
  - `3.7.2` Incremental updates: sliding the window by adding the new element on the right and subtracting the old on left.
  - `3.7.3` Why sliding windows achieve O(N): avoiding re-calculating the entire window from scratch on every step.
  - `3.7.4` Practical applications: calculating rolling average CPU usage, moving token limits, and network throughput.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Recomputing sum(window) inside every step, degenerating the algorithm back to O(N * K).
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N) time.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Rolling metrics sliding window.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.7", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N) time.", "subtopics": ["3.7.1 The sliding window paradigm: maintaining a visible slice of size K across a continuous stream of data.", "3.7.2 Incremental updates: sliding the window by adding the new element on the right and subtracting the old on left.", "3.7.3 Why sliding windows achieve O(N): avoiding re-calculating the entire window from scratch on every step.", "3.7.4 Practical applications: calculating rolling average CPU usage, moving token limits, and network throughput."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Recomputing sum(window) inside every step, degenerating the algorithm back to O(N * K).", "How does Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-8', 'phase-03-lesson-08-sliding-window-dynamic-size-subarrays', 'module-5', 8, 'Lesson 5.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.7', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.7 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 870.0, 400.0, '# Lesson 3.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.8.1` Flexible windows: expanding the right edge until a condition is met, then contracting the left edge.
  - `3.8.2` The dynamic pattern: expand right to explore, shrink left to satisfy constraints.
  - `3.8.3` Tracking window state: keeping character frequency counts or running sums inside the window.
  - `3.8.4` Canonical problems: shortest subarray with sum >= target, longest substring without repeating characters.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Shrinking the left pointer past the right pointer or failing to update the window state dictionary.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window in O(N).
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Variable-length context buffer slicing.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.8", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window in O(N).", "subtopics": ["3.8.1 Flexible windows: expanding the right edge until a condition is met, then contracting the left edge.", "3.8.2 The dynamic pattern: expand right to explore, shrink left to satisfy constraints.", "3.8.3 Tracking window state: keeping character frequency counts or running sums inside the window.", "3.8.4 Canonical problems: shortest subarray with sum >= target, longest substring without repeating characters."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Shrinking the left pointer past the right pointer or failing to update the window state dictionary.", "How does Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-9', 'phase-03-lesson-09-prefix-sums-range-sum-query-in-o-1', 'module-5', 9, 'Lesson 5.9: Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1)', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 750.0, 450.0, '# Lesson 3.9: Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1)

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.9.1` Pre-computing cumulative sums: building an array where prefix[i] stores the sum of all elements up to i.
  - `3.9.2` Instant range queries: calculating the sum between index L and R in exact O(1) time: prefix[R] - prefix[L-1].
  - `3.9.3` Trading memory for speed: spending O(N) storage to make all future range calculations instantaneous.
  - `3.9.4` Applications: computing cumulative cost over time windows and fast 2D image box filtering.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Off-by-one errors when querying ranges that start at index 0, requiring a padded 0 at prefix[0].
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a PrefixSum class that takes an array of numbers and answers 1,000 range sum queries in O(1) each.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Rapid range query calculations.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.9: Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1)\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Build a PrefixSum class that takes an array of numbers and answers 1,000 range sum queries in O(1) e\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.9", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Build a PrefixSum class that takes an array of numbers and answers 1,000 range sum queries in O(1) each.", "subtopics": ["3.9.1 Pre-computing cumulative sums: building an array where prefix[i] stores the sum of all elements up to i.", "3.9.2 Instant range queries: calculating the sum between index L and R in exact O(1) time: prefix[R] - prefix[L-1].", "3.9.3 Trading memory for speed: spending O(N) storage to make all future range calculations instantaneous.", "3.9.4 Applications: computing cumulative cost over time windows and fast 2D image box filtering."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Off-by-one errors when querying ranges that start at index 0, requiring a padded 0 at prefix[0].", "How does Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1) scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-10', 'phase-03-lesson-10-frequency-maps-counting-elements-with-dic', 'module-5', 10, 'Lesson 5.10: Frequency Maps: Counting Elements with Dicts', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.13), Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.13), Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 810.0, 500.0, '# Lesson 3.10: Frequency Maps: Counting Elements with Dicts

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.13), Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.10.1` Counting frequencies: using hash maps to track how many times each item appears in a collection.
  - `3.10.2` The Python collections.Counter helper: counting elements in one line with high performance.
  - `3.10.3` Finding most common elements: getting top-K frequencies efficiently.
  - `3.10.4` Detecting anagrams and character distributions in text streams.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Looking up keys without default values, triggering KeyError when encountering new elements.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Given a stream of user messages, count word frequencies and return the top 5 most common words.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Stream token frequency analyzer.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.10: Frequency Maps: Counting Elements with Dicts\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Given a stream of user messages, count word frequencies and return the top 5 most common words.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.10", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Given a stream of user messages, count word frequencies and return the top 5 most common words.", "subtopics": ["3.10.1 Counting frequencies: using hash maps to track how many times each item appears in a collection.", "3.10.2 The Python collections.Counter helper: counting elements in one line with high performance.", "3.10.3 Finding most common elements: getting top-K frequencies efficiently.", "3.10.4 Detecting anagrams and character distributions in text streams."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Looking up keys without default values, triggering KeyError when encountering new elements.", "How does Frequency Maps: Counting Elements with Dicts scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb)
